In this lesson, we'll look at the substitution method for solving simultaneous equations. The idea is simple. When two equations share two unknowns, you isolate one variable in one equation, then drop that expression into the other equation in place of that variable. Suddenly you're left with just one unknown, which you can solve on its own. Once you know that value, you plug it back into either original equation to find the other variable. Substitution works brilliantly whenever a variable is already sitting nicely on its own, like y equals something, or whenever you can quickly rearrange to get it that way. It's also the key technique when a straight line meets a curve. You take the line's y expression and substitute it into the curve's equation, which collapses everything down into a single polynomial in x. From there you solve for x, then back-substitute to get y. It's a clean, step-by-step way to turn two tricky equations into one manageable one. Now let's see it in action.